UPDATE "search_executions" SET "findings_note" = 'ZERO-YIELD ON ALL THREE ROUTES, AND THIS IS THE PRIOR MOST DECISIVELY FALSIFIED. I put van der Voordt 1981 at the HIGHEST retrieval odds of the five (p~0.7 record, p~0.5 full text) on the reasoning that a named academic with an institutional repository is reachable. Open Library 0; Crossref bibliographic query returned 12.7M generic results with no match; the TU Delft repository search returned nothing parseable. THE REASONING WAS WRONG IN A WAY WORTH KEEPING: an institutional repository holds what the institution deposited, and a 1981 paper predates institutional deposit entirely. Author prominence today does not make a 1981 item reachable. Candidate 111 stays PENDING-VERIFICATION. || CORRECTED 2026-09-18: R5 MISCLASSIFICATION, CAUGHT BY THE GATE AND CORRECTED RATHER THAN WAIVED. This search was logged --target-evidence-type grey because van der Voordt 1981 has no DOI and I was working an archive route. THAT IS PRECISELY THE INFERENCE R5 FORBIDS: non-indexation in PubMed/Scopus is an INDEXING fact, not an evidence-quality fact, and a Dutch-language paper titled "Accessibility by means of ramps — Some research data from the Netherlands" describes itself as research data. Retargeted to clinical. THE ERROR IS WORTH KEEPING VISIBLE because it is the batch own thesis turned against it: this batch argued that DOI-keyed searching structurally hides the pre-1990 layer, and then used absence-of-DOI as a quality signal in the same breath. || CORRECTED 2026-09-18: target_evidence_type ''grey'' -> ''clinical'' (misclassification corrected; the replaced value is kept here because the column no longer holds it) || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: RC1/RC5 phase 2-data (DR-2026-09-26): result-payload link(s) derived from this search''s own query_text against the retrieval log, per section 2.1''s rule. (3 artefact(s)) || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: result artefact(s) added: retrieval-log/session_2026-09-18-research-batch-18-archive-route-ramp-thresh