UPDATE "search_executions" SET "findings_note" = 'THREE OF FOUR PRE-REGISTERED PREDICTIONS FALSIFIED, AND THE REASONING BEHIND THE SURVIVING ONE WAS ALSO WRONG. Derive every figure here: python3 scripts/research/mining_screen.py --ref REF-01005 --compare. P1a FALSIFIED -- 38 references, not the 60-plus predicted. P1b FALSIFIED -- slope-strict scores 1-2, below the predicted 3-12 band. NOTE AGAINST MYSELF: my own pre-registered falsification condition said ''falsified if 0 or >=20'', which 1-2 does not trigger. The point prediction failed and the threshold I set to catch it was too lax to fire. Recording both rather than hiding behind the looser test. P1c FALSIFIED -- slope-loose scores 1-3 against slope-strict''s 1-2, nowhere near 3x. THE REASON I GAVE WAS WRONG IN A WAY WORTH KEEPING: I predicted the bare stem accessib would fire on everything because the report is an accessibility report. It fires on almost nothing, because the BIBLIOGRAPHY is not an accessibility bibliography -- it is a HUMAN FACTORS AND ERGONOMICS bibliography (Damon, Diffrient, Kroemer, McCormick, Murrell, Poulton, Human Factors and Applied Ergonomics as the recurring venues). A 1979 federal accessibility report drew its authority from ergonomics, not from a disability literature, and that is a finding about the period rather than about the screen. P1d CONFIRMED BUT WITH A DISCREPANCY THAT MATTERS MORE THAN THE PREDICTION: both appear, and the Templer entry is 1974 on STAIR SHAPE, while Table 13 footnote c cites ''Templer, 1977''. Templer 1977 IS NOT IN THIS BIBLIOGRAPHY. THE YIELD IS A FLOOR, NOT A COUNT: the OCR is damaged, so an entry whose title is mangled below the screen''s terms is a false negative, and the band''s ceiling says how much could be hidden. TWO SLOPE-TITLED ENTRIES: Elmer C.D. (candidate 110, scores in the conservative band -- ''ramp'' is legible) and Corlett et al. ''Ramps or stairs'' in Applied Ergonomics 3 (ceiling only -- ''stairs'' is legible, ''Ramps'' is not, newly staged this pass). WALTER DOES NOT SCORE AT ALL, and that is the most instructive result: it is the single most slope-relevant item in the list, and its title in this bibliography carries no slope term because ''Part 3: Ramp gradients'' is a part title. A title screen cannot see it. Recorded so no later reader reads a low yield as a thin literature. || CORRECTED 2026-09-20: THE PREMISE OF THIS PASS WAS FALSE AND THE YIELD IT REPORTED WAS WRONG. Found by an adversarial pass over this same batch. This search recorded that the ED184280 scan was unreadable -- "no OCR engine is available in this environment (no tesseract, no pdftotext), so this text layer is the best that exists here" -- and transcribed 38 entries with authors marked illegible, years marked unresolvable and one slope term marked absent. THE PAGES ARE JBIG2 IMAGES INSIDE THE PDF AND THEY RENDER LEGIBLY IN ABOUT A SECOND. pypdf reads a TEXT LAYER; when that layer is mush it says the layer is mush and nothing whatever about the document. I read the mush as a property of the page and then reasoned at length, carefully, and wrongly about the consequences. WHAT THE PAGES ACTUALLY SAY, re-transcribed this batch from renders at 130 dpi and persisted as the superseding artefact c9361532746ffec9.json: Birren J.E. Psychology of aging 1964 (recorded as "C[u]rren" and filed at alphabetical position C, which then required a bogus note claiming Brattgard was out of order -- the list is in strict order and the misreading invented the anomaly); Corlett, Hutcheson, DeLugan and Rogozenski, RAMPS OR STAIRS, Applied ergonomics 3:4 pp 195-201 1972 (the word Ramps was called illegible); Elmer C.D. ... University of IL, 1957 (the year was called unresolvable); Jones J.C. Fitting for action (recorded as AUTHOR LOST TO THE SCAN); Kidwell A.M.; Kira A.; Nichols P.J.R.; Harrigan J.E.; Leonard J.A.; Steinfeld, EDWARD (the OCR read Eckard and I recorded Eckard as the reading); Templer J.A. ... New York: COLUMBIA UNIVERSITY 1974; Walter, F. ... 1971 (I called the initial NOT an F on its face and the year OCR-damaged, and staged a candidate partly to chase the unexplained initial -- there was nothing to explain). THE YIELD IS CORRECTED FROM A BAND TO A POINT: slope-strict is 2, not the 1-2 reported, because Corlett scores in the floor once the title is read rather than guessed. Re-derive: python3 scripts/research/mining_screen.py --ref REF-01005 --compare, which now reports provenance READ rather than SCRAPED. WHAT SURVIVES UNCHANGED: 38 entries; the ANSI-to-Webb alphabetical span is complete; Walter still carries no slope term in its title, so the title-screen false negative recorded for it stands and is the pass real finding. THE MECHANISM IS FIXED, not just the reading: scripts/research/page_image.py renders a persisted PDF page so a session can look at the scan, pymupdf is declared in the check registry batteries, and mining_screen now distinguishes READ from SCRAPED and lets a superseding transcription win over the one it replaces. || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: RC5 phase 2-data (DR-2026-09-26 5.2d): mined_ref_id derived -- this row''s own query_text names exactly one REF id that exists in evidence_sources. || RC1/RC5 phase 2-data (DR-2026-09-26): result-payload link(s) derived from this search''s own query_text against the retrieval log, per section 2.1''s rule. (4 artefact(s)) || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: mined_ref_id set to ''REF-01005'' || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: result artefact(s) added: retrieval-log/session_2026-09-20-research-batch-19-pre1990-chain-steinfeld-walter-templer/08263082ebdda8eb.json, retrieval-log/session_2026-09-20-research-batch-19-pre1990-chain-steinfeld-walter-templer/5dd866a236fb5978.p0055.png, retrieval-log/session_2026-09-20-research-batch-19-pre1990-chain-steinfeld-walter-templer/5dd866a236fb5978.p0162.png, retrieval-log/session_2026-09-20-research-batch-19-pre1990-chain-steinfeld-walter-templer/c9361532746ffec9.json', "mined_ref_id" = 'REF-01005' WHERE "exec_id" = 88;
UPDATE "search_executions" SET "findings_note" = 'THE ASSIGNED ROUTE IS CLOSED AND A DIFFERENT ONE ANSWERED. JISC IS UNREACHABLE FROM THIS ENVIRONMENT: both queries returned HTTP 403 carrying a Cloudflare ''Just a moment...'' interstitial. NOTHING IS ESTABLISHED ABOUT WHAT JISC HOLDS -- this is a BLOCKED ROUTE, not a null, and it is the second library discovery catalogue in two batches to fail this way (HathiTrust, batch 18). Candidate 109 named Jisc as its NEXT ROUTE, UNTRIED; that route is now closed and the candidate should stop pointing at it. P2a FALSIFIED AS WRITTEN, CONFIRMED IN SUBSTANCE: Jisc returned nothing, but the underlying expectation -- that an authoritative catalogue record exists and is reachable -- held, via Open Library searched BY PUBLISHER rather than by title. That is the query-shape lesson of R14 arriving as a positive: batch 18 searched Open Library for the TITLE and got 0; searching for the PUBLISHER returns 99 DLF records and the target sits among them. P2b CORRECT: no full text, and none of these routes could have supplied it. WHAT IS NEW, AND IT IS NOT THE RECORD: batch 18 ALREADY extracted Open Library OL4653562M in full -- Felix Walter, DLF, London, 1971, 60 pp -- and candidate 109 already carries it. I briefly believed batch 18 had missed it and checked before recording that; it had not. What this pass adds is the HOLDINGS LAYER the earlier note does not carry: ISBN-10 0901908053, LCCN 77502052, OCLC 222208, LC class NA2545.P5 D5, Dewey 729/.2, single edition, source record marc:marc_loc_2016 (a Library of Congress MARC record, which is why the 1971 date is authoritative rather than inferred). THOSE IDENTIFIERS ARE THE POINT: GAP-026 says what is needed is document delivery, not another search. An OCLC number and an LCCN convert ''find Walter 1971'' into ''request OCLC 222208'', which is an ILL action a human can take. GOOGLE BOOKS IS NOT MERELY EXHAUSTED: the 429 body reads quota_limit_value ''0'' -- a ZERO daily allocation, not a quota that resets. Batch 18 recorded it as a daily quota; two days later the limit is still 0, so it is structurally unavailable here and no future batch should spend a query testing for a reset. OPENALEX AND CORE both 429''d on a shared-IP daily budget (OpenAlex retryAfter ~75000s), though one later OpenAlex call in this same batch succeeded -- the budget is intermittent, not uniformly closed. WELLCOME: HTTP 200, 0 results, a genuine null from a reachable index, but Wellcome is a health-and-medicine library and an architectural report is out of its scope, so this is R14 ''wrong index'', not evidence of absence. || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: RC1/RC5 phase 2-data (DR-2026-09-26): result-payload link(s) derived from this search''s own query_text against the retrieval log, per section 2.1''s rule. (2 artefact(s)) || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: result artefact(s) added: retrieval-log/session_2026-09-20-research-batch-19-pre1990-chain-steinfeld-walter-templer/7feee04dacc3ae66.json, retrieval-log/session_2026-09-20-research-batch-19-pre1990-chain-steinfeld-walter-templer/8987c52dc3dba896.json' WHERE "exec_id" = 89;
UPDATE "search_executions" SET "findings_note" = 'TEMPLER 1977 DOES NOT RESOLVE TO ANY INDEXED ITEM, AND THE SURNAME IS NOW SETTLED AS John A. Templer, A STAIRS RESEARCHER. THE NEGATIVE IS WELL-FORMED, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES IT USABLE (R14). ERIC author:\"Templer\" returns 33 records -- a healthy result set, so the query shape is sound -- and NOT ONE is architectural: they are Bill Templer (education/politics), Lois Templer, Andrew J. Templer (management), Donald I. Templer (psychology, death-anxiety scales), Sally Templer. ERIC \"Templer\" AND \"stairs\" returns 0. A well-formed author query returning 33 and a topic conjunction returning 0 is GENUINE ABSENCE IN ERIC, not a query-shape failure -- and ERIC being an education index, absence there says nothing about the built-environment literature. P3b CORRECT AT 0.2. OpenAlex raw_author_name Templer + year 1977 returns 10, all of them Jerry W. Templer (ENT surgery), Donald I. Templer (psychology), Andrew J. Templer (management) or J.C. Templer. NO J.A. TEMPLER IN 1977 IN EITHER INDEX. P3a FALSIFIED: TRID served a 200 with a results page, and the page does not carry a single /View/ record id -- the markup is JS-rendered, so what came back is a shell. ''Templer'' appears 5 times in the body but no record is extractable. NOTHING IS ESTABLISHED ABOUT WHAT TRID HOLDS; treat this as a blocked route like Jisc, not as a null. WHAT DID RESOLVE, VIA CROSSREF ON THE DISSERTATION TITLE REF-01005''S OWN BIBLIOGRAPHY CARRIES: John A. Templer''s indexed corpus is stairs, end to end -- ''An analysis of the behavior of stair users'', John A Templer, Gary M Mullet, John Archea, Stephen T Margulis, 1978, NBS internal report, DOI 10.6028/nbs.ir.78-1554; ''The Forgiving Stair'', 1984, Human Factors Society; ''The Soft Stair: Falls Induced by a Laboratory Stair'', 1989. Together with the 1974 dissertation ''Stair shape and human movement'' that REF-01005''s bibliography actually lists, that is FOUR Templer items spanning fifteen years and NOT ONE OF THEM IS ABOUT RAMPS, and not one is dated 1977. P3e ANSWERED IN AN UNEXPECTED DIRECTION: the ambiguity is not between several 1977 Templers, it is that there is no 1977 Templer at all and the nearest neighbours are 1974 and 1978. STILL NOT GUESSED, PER THE ASSIGNMENT: the 1978 NBS report is one year off and would be a plausible preprint-dated citation, and the 1974 dissertation is the one the report itself lists. Either could be footnote c''s referent and this pass does not choose. THE STRUCTURAL FINDING IS INDEPENDENT OF WHICH ONE IT IS: Table 13''s footnote c warrants the 1:8 and 1:10 rampway bands on two named parties, and one of them has no ramp research to his name. || CORRECTED 2026-09-20: RETRACTED: THE UNIVERSAL NEGATIVE IN THIS NOTE IS FALSE. This pass concluded that "John A. Templer corpus is stairs, end to end", "FOUR ITEMS ACROSS FIFTEEN YEARS AND NOT ONE OF THEM IS ABOUT RAMPS", and that "one of them has no ramp research to his name". Found false by an adversarial pass over this same batch, and the route that finds it is one this session used the same hour for a different target. TEMPLER HAS SUBSTANTIAL RAMP RESEARCH: Templer, John A., Provisions for elderly and handicapped pedestrians, FHWA-RD-79-1 (Jan 1979), -2 and -3 (May 1980), Georgia Institute of Technology Pedestrian Research Laboratory, contract DOT-FH-11-8504, OCLC 5844243, full text on Internet Archive. Volume 3 contains a Part I titled SHORT RAMPS, TACTILE SURFACES AND WHEELCHAIR DIMENSIONS opening with "A Study of Short Ramps"; 32 gradient-rating tables (manual wheelchair users, electric wheelchair users, cane users, ambulant disabled, able-bodied, ascent and descent); and TABLE 20, "Ramp Gradient Recommendations", recommending against curb heights of 3, 6 and 9 inches across gradients 1:8, 1:10, 1:12 and 1:16. Derive it: curl the archive.org advancedsearch endpoint for creator:Templer AND (pedestrian OR ramp OR handicapped) -- 3 results -- then count ramp in provisionsforeld00temp_0_djvu.txt, which returns 998. HOW THE ERROR WAS MADE, because the shape matters more than the fact. (1) THE QUERY THAT "RESOLVED HIS CORPUS" CONTAINED THE WORD STAIR. It was query.bibliographic=Templer+stair+shape+human+movement. A query containing stair returning stair papers is the query shape, not the corpus -- which is R14, the rule this same note invokes against ERIC two sentences earlier, applied to everyone except myself. (2) archive.org was never pointed at Templer, though this batch used exactly that endpoint for Walter at 03:10. (3) ERIC returned numFound 33 and the request carried rows=25, so 25 docs were examined and EIGHT WERE NEVER SEEN; "33 records, none architectural" overstates what was looked at, and results_screened=43 is a hand-typed figure the artefact contradicts. WHAT THIS DOES TO THE BATCH CONCLUSIONS: candidate 115 correction stands as far as it goes (the bibliography Templer is the 1974 Columbia stairs dissertation, and footnote c cites 1977) but its reasoning is void; candidate 117 stages the wrong neighbour; and the inference that "the priority of retrieving Templer drops" is INVERTED -- Table 20 recommends over 1:8, 1:10, 1:12 and 1:16, the SAME FOUR BANDS as Steinfeld Table 13, and footnote c marks the two Steinfeld did not test. That is now the strongest available lead on what footnote c means. Candidate 125 stages it. STILL NOT GUESSED: Volume 3 is dated May 1980 and footnote c says 1977; the project field tests ran to Sep 1977, so an interim report of the same contract is plausible and unverified. || ADMISSION LINKED 2026-09-25 by session_2026-09-25-research-batch-20-audit-cluster-templer-fhwa: REF-01008 (candidate 125): Candidate 125 was surfaced by this search and admitted as REF-01008 in batch 20 by retrieving its locator. No verb could attach an admission to an already-logged search, so the edge was missing and research_protocol_audit reported REF-01008 as admitted by no search. Written with link-admission (GAP-046), which refuses unless the candidate row records both ends, as S01 requires. || CORRECTED 2026-09-26: BATCH 20''s ADMISSION LINE ABOVE OVERSTATES THE PROVENANCE. It says ''Candidate 125 was surfaced by this search''. This search''s logged query names seven routes (ERIC, OpenAlex, Crossref, TRID) and not Internet Archive, where batch 19''s adversarial pass found the FHWA report (GAP-037''s 2026-09-20 text; the correction above on this note records the find). The edge (90, REF-01008) copies the candidate''s filing on this exec and is not independent evidence of it; results_admitted here is deliberately NOT raised to meet it. See GAP-050. || ADMISSION UNLINKED 2026-09-26 by session_2026-09-25-research-batch-20-audit-cluster-templer-fhwa: REF-01008: Owner ruling 2026-09-26, recorded on contact (CLAUDE.md rule 0): fix GAP-050 before merge, following batch 17''s candidates 107/108. This edge copied a proxy attribution: the candidate that became REF-01008 was filed on this search because the step that surfaced it was never logged. It is moved to that step, now logged as a backfill (GAP-050). || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: RC1/RC5 phase 2-data (DR-2026-09-26): result-payload link(s) derived from this search''s own query_text against the retrieval log, per section 2.1''s rule. (4 artefact(s)) || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: result artefact(s) added: retrieval-log/session_2026-09-20-research-batch-19-pre1990-chain-steinfeld-walter-templer/145fd1374b96d1ae.html, retrieval-log/session_2026-09-20-research-batch-19-pre1990-chain-steinfeld-walter-templer/9acaa54f446703ed.json, retrieval-log/session_2026-09-20-research-batch-19-pre1990-chain-steinfeld-walter-templer/b7520ecbedff13a8.json, retrieval-log/session_2026-09-20-research-batch-19-pre1990-chain-steinfeld-walter-templer/cdf823992cae2556.json' WHERE "exec_id" = 90;
UPDATE "search_executions" SET "findings_note" = 'P6a CONFIRMED AND IT WAS THE LOW-PROBABILITY BRANCH. Admitted REF-01006 -- Raghuram, Verma, Lavalekar, Virk, Bhan and Singh 2026, ''Participatory accessibility audits as a tool for disability-inclusive health systems: Findings from two Indian states'', Dialogues in Health, doi 10.1016/j.dialog.2026.100342, PMC13543994, open access, full text retrieved as JATS XML and persisted. CO-1 AT THREE LEVELS, EVIDENCED IN THE RETRIEVED BYTES PER D-0178 RATHER THAN INFERRED FROM VENUE: research team composed primarily of persons with disabilities; a Community Advisory Board of disability rights advocates; and ALL field investigators persons with disabilities, audits run with a DPO. Named as Community Based Participatory Research, closing on ''Nothing about us, Without us''. IT STATES A GRADIENT AND THE STATEMENT MUST NOT BE OVER-READ: 1:12 is a yardstick taken from the Harmonised Guidelines and Standards for Universal Accessibility in India (2021), not a figure this study derived, so it is NOT Co-1 evidence that 1:12 is right. What it measures is compliance: 20 of 35 government hospitals (57.1 percent) at 1:12 or gentler, 17 of 24 in Madhya Pradesh, 3 of 11 in Goa. Recorded as an R7 failure finding. P6b UNTESTED BY THIS PASS RATHER THAN CONFIRMED, and the distinction matters: every query here was date-unbounded or post-2000, and none interrogated the pre-1990 layer, so nothing was learned about whether disabled-authored ramp work exists in that period. It remains an open question and this pass should not be cited as having settled it. P6c NOT THE RESULT, and I would have accepted it too readily: I pre-committed to reporting a silence as a finding, and a source turned up instead. Recording that the low-probability branch fired is the honest outcome, and it is a caution against the shape of P6c -- a prior that makes a null valuable is a prior that makes looking harder feel unnecessary. THE JURISDICTION IS NEW: India, on a slug whose Co-1 rows were both Japanese. ERIC returned 0 for ''wheelchair users'' AND ''ramp'', consistent with batch 18 finding ERIC topically thin on built-environment work while holding ED184280 by deposit. || ADMISSION LINKED 2026-09-25 by session_2026-09-25-research-batch-20-audit-cluster-templer-fhwa: REF-01007 (candidate 124): Candidate 124 was surfaced by this search and admitted as REF-01007 in batch 20 by retrieving its locator. No verb could attach an admission to an already-logged search, so the edge was missing and research_protocol_audit reported REF-01007 as admitted by no search. Written with link-admission (GAP-046), which refuses unless the candidate row records both ends, as S01 requires. || CORRECTED 2026-09-26: BATCH 20''s ADMISSION LINE ABOVE OVERSTATES THE PROVENANCE. It says ''Candidate 124 was surfaced by this search''. Batch 19''s own record (sessions/session_2026-09-20-research-batch-19-pre1990-chain-steinfeld-walter-templer.md, section 13) says Pinto 2021 -- and Garg 2024, candidate 123 -- were found in REF-01006''s REFERENCE LIST, the source this search admitted, not in this search''s results; that backward step was never logged as a search. Candidates 123 and 124 were filed on this exec as the nearest logged search. The edge (91, REF-01007) copies that filing and is not independent evidence of it; results_admitted here is deliberately NOT raised to meet it. See GAP-050. || ADMISSION UNLINKED 2026-09-26 by session_2026-09-25-research-batch-20-audit-cluster-templer-fhwa: REF-01007: Owner ruling 2026-09-26, recorded on contact (CLAUDE.md rule 0): fix GAP-050 before merge, following batch 17''s candidates 107/108. This edge copied a proxy attribution: the candidate that became REF-01007 was filed on this search because the step that surfaced it was never logged. It is moved to that step, now logged as a backfill (GAP-050). || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: RC1/RC5 phase 2-data (DR-2026-09-26): result-payload link(s) derived from this search''s own query_text against the retrieval log, per section 2.1''s rule. (4 artefact(s)) || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: result artefact(s) added: retrieval-log/session_2026-09-20-research-batch-19-pre1990-chain-steinfeld-walter-templer/156fd66759c674ec.json, retrieval-log/session_2026-09-20-research-batch-19-pre1990-chain-steinfeld-walter-templer/27ec6599a8b1166f.json, retrieval-log/session_2026-09-20-research-batch-19-pre1990-chain-steinfeld-walter-templer/2d6ec7cd5f7dfc96.json, retrieval-log/session_2026-09-20-research-batch-19-pre1990-chain-steinfeld-walter-templer/8c4dc0435df5f36e.json' WHERE "exec_id" = 91;
UPDATE "search_executions" SET "findings_note" = 'DERIVE THE SCREEN YIELD, DO NOT QUOTE IT: python3 scripts/research/mining_screen.py --ref REF-01007 --compare (slope-strict and slope-strict-plus-motion both 0 on this run; slope-loose fires on the accessibility stem). THE ZERO IS THE KNOWN BLIND SPOT, NOT A THIN LIST: reading the 32 titles and then fetching four abstracts (persisted b7b928f29b468566.json) found the audit literature a title screen cannot see. STAGED: Martins et al. 2016 (Ciencia e Saude Coletiva, doi 10.1590/1413-812320152110.20052016), 90 Family Health Units in Joao Pessoa audited against ABNT NBR 9050 -- its abstract reports ''only 47.8% have a wheelchair ramp, of these 30.0% have maximum slope'', the first MEASURED-SLOPE compliance figure this pass has seen, wording ambiguous in translation; Mudrick et al. 2012 (Disability and Health Journal, doi 10.1016/j.dhjo.2012.02.002), 2,389 California primary care facilities on a 55-item on-site instrument -- exterior access ''generally met the access criteria'', whether ramp slope is an item is not in the abstract. NOT STAGED, read and set aside: Nischith, Bhargava and Akshaya 2018 (J Family Med Prim Care, 67 PHCs in Dakshina Kannada, AIC checklist) reports ramp PRESENCE (60 of 67) in its abstract, the same shape as REF-01007; Frost et al. 2015 (JRRD, 30 clinics, ADA site assessments) reports restrooms and exam rooms, not ramps. harm_finding=1: every audit read reports inadequacy. || CORRECTED 2026-09-25: CORRECTION TO THIS ROW''S prior_expectation, SAME BATCH. It says batch 19''s warning about audits and title screens was ''carried into priors.md P1''. It was not: P1a-P1f are about what candidates 123 and 124 contain, and none mentions the title-screen blind spot. The honest reading of the prior field is its first sentence -- no per-pass prior was written -- and nothing more. || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: RC5 phase 2-data (DR-2026-09-26 5.2d): mined_ref_id derived -- this row''s own query_text names exactly one REF id that exists in evidence_sources. || RC1/RC5 phase 2-data (DR-2026-09-26): result-payload link(s) derived from this search''s own query_text against the retrieval log, per section 2.1''s rule. (1 artefact(s)) || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: mined_ref_id set to ''REF-01007'' || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: result artefact(s) added: retrieval-log/session_2026-09-25-research-batch-20-audit-cluster-templer-fhwa/265e92157feb3c35.json', "mined_ref_id" = 'REF-01007' WHERE "exec_id" = 95;
UPDATE "search_executions" SET "findings_note" = 'BODY UNREACHABLE FROM THIS ENVIRONMENT BY EVERY ROUTE TRIED; ABSTRACT AND METADATA RETRIEVED. results_found=1 is the one record (the article) located; it could not be read past its abstract. Outcomes, each persisted: Crossref 200; Europe PMC core 200 (abstract); Europe PMC fullTextXML HTTP 500; NCBI efetch 200 but front matter only, with the comment ''The publisher of this article does not allow downloading of the full text in XML form'' and pmc-prop-open-access ''no''; PMC article page and PMC /pdf/ both a reCAPTCHA interstitial; Europe PMC PDF render 403 (Cloudflare); ScienceDirect 403 (Cloudflare error 1000); Unpaywall and OpenAlex both name PMC as the only location. So the item is FREE-TO-READ, not OA-licensed, and the free copy sits behind a bot check. That is a correction to batch 19 and to the runbook, both of which called it retrievable here. Not admitted; candidate 123 re-described accordingly. The Unpaywall request carried the email the project''s earlier Unpaywall calls used, which is the user''s own address -- noted so it is not repeated. || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: RC1/RC5 phase 2-data (DR-2026-09-26): result-payload link(s) derived from this search''s own query_text against the retrieval log, per section 2.1''s rule. (4 artefact(s)) || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: result artefact(s) added: retrieval-log/session_2026-09-25-research-batch-20-audit-cluster-templer-fhwa/1b5af0921feb3c76.html, retrieval-log/session_2026-09-25-research-batch-20-audit-cluster-templer-fhwa/5f203627dcc60cac.json, retrieval-log/session_2026-09-25-research-batch-20-audit-cluster-templer-fhwa/7c7169d47ce47541.json, retrieval-log/session_2026-09-25-research-batch-20-audit-cluster-templer-fhwa/adf5e627d9b7d7a9.html' WHERE "exec_id" = 96;
UPDATE "search_executions" SET "findings_note" = 'THE WHOLE REFERENCE LIST IS FIVE ENTRIES AND NONE IS NEW. DERIVE: python3 scripts/research/mining_screen.py --ref REF-01008 --compare (provenance READ). (1) Dixon, Charles E., 1957 Illinois PhD dissertation -- already candidate 110, whose author is contested with REF-01005''s ''Elmer, C.D.''; this list adds nothing to settle it. (2) ANSI A117.1, printed with a 1977 date and a Syracuse University imprint -- the standard REF-01005 was commissioned to improve; a code, not a study. (3) Walter, Felix, Four Architectural Movement Studies for the Wheelchair and ''Ambuland'' Disabled, DLF, London, 1971 -- already candidate 109, exhausted under the 2026-09-25 owner ruling. (4) Herms, Elias and Robbins 1976, guidestrips for visually handicapped pedestrians -- tactile guidance, off parameter 3. (5) Snyder and Knoblauch 1971, pedestrian safety countermeasures -- vehicle conflict, off parameter 3. NO CANDIDATE STAGED: the two on-parameter entries are already staged and the other three are off it. FORWARD NOT RUN: no citation index holds a 1980 FHWA report as a citable node here, and R2''s mandatory scope is T1-T2. || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: RC5 phase 2-data (DR-2026-09-26 5.2d): mined_ref_id derived -- this row''s own query_text names exactly one REF id that exists in evidence_sources. || RC1/RC5 phase 2-data (DR-2026-09-26): result-payload link(s) derived from this search''s own query_text against the retrieval log, per section 2.1''s rule. (10 artefact(s)) || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: mined_ref_id set to ''REF-01008'' || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: result artefact(s) added: retrieval-log/session_2026-09-25-research-batch-20-audit-cluster-templer-fhwa/75c991f61483c1ac.json, retrieval-log/session_2026-09-25-research-batch-20-audit-cluster-templer-fhwa/db21e48738335d82.p0002.png, retrieval-log/session_2026-09-25-research-batch-20-audit-cluster-templer-fhwa/db21e48738335d82.p0023.png, retrieval-log/session_2026-09-25-research-batch-20-audit-cl