UPDATE "search_candidates" SET "notes" = 'SURFACED WHILE FAILING TO FIND SOMETHING ELSE, which is the honest provenance. Crossref, queried on the dissertation title REF-01005''s bibliography carries, returned John A. Templer''s indexed corpus instead: this 1978 NBS report, ''The Forgiving Stair'' (1984, Human Factors Society, doi 10.1177/154193128402800118), and ''The Soft Stair: Falls Induced by a Laboratory Stair'' (1989, doi 10.1518/107118189786758148). Full author list here: John A Templer, Gary M Mullet, John Archea, Stephen T Margulis. WHAT MAKES IT CHEAP TO RESOLVE: NBS is now NIST and its internal reports are public, so unlike Walter 1971 and Elmer 1957 this one is very likely retrievable in full without document delivery -- nvlpubs.nist.gov is the route and it has not been tried. IF RETRIEVED, THE QUESTION TO ASK IS NARROW: does it contain any rampway slope or length recommendation at all? If yes, footnote c probably means this and the 1:8/1:10 warrant is identified. If no -- which is what its title predicts -- then footnote c''s Templer is the 1974 dissertation or an item outside every index reached, and either way the warrant for those two bands rests on stair research. THAT QUESTION IS WORTH ONE RETRIEVAL AND NOT MORE. || RESOLVED 2026-09-20 (R15, re-described from the source): R15 CORRECTION: THE PREMISE FOR STAGING THIS ROW IS GONE. It was staged as "the nearest RESOLVABLE item to Table 13 footnote c" on the reasoning that John A. Templer had no ramp research and that a 1978 stairs report was therefore the closest thing to the cited 1977 item. That reasoning is retracted on exec 90: Templer FHWA-RD-79-1/-2/-3 is dedicated ramp research and is candidate 125. THIS ROW IS NOW WHAT IT ALWAYS WAS ON ITS FACE -- a study of STAIR users, off parameter 3, with no claim to make about ramp gradient. Keep it PENDING rather than retiring it: NBS IR 78-1554 is also free on Internet Archive (identifier analysisofbehavi7815temp), it is by the same author in the same period, and if the FHWA trail fails to identify footnote c referent this is still a node worth reading. But it is no longer the first call, and the question this row was staged to answer should be put to candidate 125 instead. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source): REHOME TO stair-ramp-threshold-biomechanics-accessibility (an ACTIVE slug in the slugs table), NOT ADMITTED ON PARAMETER 3. Retrieved in full from NIST (nbsir78-1554.pdf, persisted) and read -- the text layer is sound, and the low-text pages were checked. It is what its title says and what this row''s own staging note predicted: a behavioural and accident study of STAIR users for the Consumer Product Safety Commission, with performance statements on risers, treads, handrails, lighting and visual orientation. It contains NO RAMP SLOPE OR LENGTH RECOMMENDATION: every ''ramp'' is incidental (scope, alternatives to stairs, not mistaking a stair for a ramp, falls on stairs versus falls on ramps, and citations), and every ''gradient'' is the report''s ''orientation gradient'', a perceptual measure, not a slope. R15 CORRECTIONS: the staged title gives 1978; the title page reads ''February 1976, Issued November 1978'' -- the report was written two years before it was issued. The staging premise (nearest resolvable item to footnote c''s ''Templer, 1977'') was already retracted on 2026-09-20 in favour of candidate 125, now REF-01008. WHAT THE READ ADDS TO THE FOOTNOTE-c QUESTION, WITHOUT ANSWERING IT: the report cites ''Templer, J., Stairs and Ramps, Dowden, Hutchinson and Ross, Stroudsburg, PA., forthcoming'' six times -- a Templer book on ramps announced in a 1976-78 federal report. That is a third possible referent for ''Templer, 1977'' beside the 1974 dissertation and the FHWA ramp study, and it is NOT chosen here (candidate 115 stays PENDING). Rehome, not out-of-scope: stair-user behaviour, risers, treads and handrails are that slug''s subject, and a later pass there should read it as primary stair evidence. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source): CORRECTION TO THE RE-DESCRIPTION ABOVE, SAME BATCH. It says ''the report was written two years before it was issued''. The title page prints ''February 1976'' and ''Issued November 1978''; it does not say what the earlier date marks (drafting, contract completion, a period covered). Recorded as printed, without the inference. Everything else in the rehoming stands. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source; suggested_slug accessible-circulation-geometry -> stair-ramp-threshold-biomechanics-accessibility): TYPED COLUMN BROUGHT INTO LINE WITH THE REHOMING. The re-description above rehomed this row to stair-ramp-threshold-biomechanics-accessibility while suggested_slug still named accessible-circulation-geometry, where it was found -- prose and column disagreeing, found by an independent adversarial pass (finding #5). resolve-candidate could not write suggested_slug; the flag was added in batch 20 (GAP-045) and is used here. The same pass confirmed the content call: ''gradient'' in this report is always an orientation gradient, and there is no ramp recommendation.

