UPDATE "search_candidates" SET "notes" = 'harm_finding=1 on the same reading as its sibling -- the title asks whether people are being DENIED a right. RETRIEVAL IS CHEAP AND THE ROUTE IS KNOWN, which is what separates this cluster from everything else this batch chased: Walter 1971, Elmer 1957 and Templer are all behind GAP-041''s closed catalogue stratum, while these are DOI-bearing open-access articles from the deposit-based indexes that DO work here. A batch that wants measured ramp gradients rather than archival provenance should start here. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source; admitted as REF-01007): ADMITTED AS REF-01007, T3 (clinical, lower_control), AND TWO THINGS THE STAGED ROW SAID ARE CORRECTED FROM THE SOURCE. Read in full from the Europe PMC JATS XML (PMC7999795, persisted as retrieval-log/<session>/2c65a16afca1967b.xml); bibliographic fields from the Crossref payload. (1) THE TIER GUESS WAS WRONG. The row guessed tier 2 because ''a national facility audit is closer to systematic evidence than to grey primary''. It is neither: it is a SECONDARY ANALYSIS of a cross-sectional census (PMAQ-AB first cycle, 2012) of 38,812 public primary health care facilities, which the ladder places at T3 clinical lower_control. Scale does not make a census a synthesis. (2) THE HOPE THAT IT ''TABULATES MEASURED GRADIENTS'' IS FALSE, AND THE SOURCE SAYS WHY. The instrument is 22 yes/no items; the ramp item is ''Does the health facility have access ramp?'' -- presence only, no gradient. The authors give the reason in their introduction: tools that require ''measurement of slopes of ramps and widths of doors'' are complex, ''As a consequence, few large-scale accessibility audits have been conducted''. So the one national audit in this cluster bought its scale by not measuring gradient. Filed on parameter 3 as claim_type ABSENT, which is the honest reading and is itself a finding about the audit literature. WHAT STANDS FROM THE STAGED ROW: harm_finding=1 -- access ramp present at 44 percent of facilities, handrail at the entrance 8 percent, accessible entrance 35 percent, all worse in the poorer North/Northeast and in small municipalities; the authors call it a violation of the right to health under the UNCRPD and Brazilian law. R13 graded PROXY: nobody disabled took part in the measurement in any role. NOT Co-1 on any reading. Backward pass run on admission (exec 95); it staged candidates 129 and 130. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source; admitted as REF-01007): CORRECTION TO THE RE-DESCRIPTION ABOVE, SAME BATCH. It says ''The authors give the reason in their introduction'' and that ''the one national audit in this cluster bought its scale by not measuring gradient''. Over-read: the introduction explains why large audits are rare in general (slope and width measurement make tools complex), and the paper is a secondary analysis of a Ministry of Health census whose instrument its authors did not design; no sentence says why that instrument asks about ramp presence and not gradient. Presence-only and absent on parameter 3 stand; the causal claim is withdrawn. Extraction 59 carries the same correction.

